IS  - 2072-1439 (Linking)
VI  - 6
IP  - 4
DP  - 2014 Apr
TI  - Radiation dose effect in locally advanced non-small cell lung cancer.
PG  - 336-47
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2014.01.23 [doi]
AB  - Radiation is the foundation of treatment for locally advanced non-small cell lung 
      cancer (NSCLC), and as such, optimal radiation dose is essential for successful 
      treatment. This article will briefly review biological considerations of 
      radiation dose and their effect in the context of three-dimensional conformal 
      radiation therapy (3D-CRT) including intensity modulated radiation therapy (IMRT) 
      and stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) for NSCLC. It will focus on 
      literature review and discussions regarding radiation dose effect in locally 
      advanced NSCLC including potential severe and lethal toxicities of high dose 
      radiation given with concurrent chemotherapy. Potential new approaches for 
      delivering safe and effective doses by individualizing treatment based on 
      functional imaging are being applied in studies such as the PET boost trial and 
      RTOG1106. The RTOG concept of delivering high dose radiation to the more 
      resistant tumors with the use of isotoxic dose prescription and adaptive planning 
      will also be discussed in detail.
