TI  - Imaging techniques for tumour delineation and heterogeneity quantification of 
      lung cancer: overview of current possibilities.
PG  - 319-27
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2013.08.62 [doi]
AB  - Imaging techniques for the characterization and delineation of primary lung 
      tumours and lymph nodes are a prerequisite for adequate radiotherapy. Numerous 
      imaging modalities have been proposed for this purpose, but only computed 
      tomography (CT) and FDG-PET have been implemented in clinical routine. Hypoxia 
      PET, dynamic contrast-enhanced CT (DCE-CT), dual energy CT (DECT) and 
      (functional) magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) hold promise for the future. 
      Besides information on the primary tumour, these techniques can be used for 
      quantification of tissue heterogeneity and response. In the future, treatment 
      strategies may be designed which are based on imaging techniques to optimize 
      individual treatment.
