TI  - Accelerated dose escalation with proton beam therapy for non-small cell lung 
      cancer.
PG  - 348-55
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2013.11.07 [doi]
AB  - Local tumor control remains challenging in many cases of non-small cell lung 
      cancer (NSCLC), particularly those that involve large or centrally located 
      tumors. Concurrent chemotherapy and radiation can maximize tumor control and 
      survival for patients with locally advanced disease, but a substantial proportion 
      of such patients cannot tolerate this therapy, and sequential chemoradiation 
      regimens or radiation given alone at conventionally fractionated doses produces 
      suboptimal results. An alternative approach is the use of hypofractionated proton 
      beam therapy (PBT). The energy distribution of protons can be exploited to reduce 
      involuntary irradiation of normal tissues, particularly the low-dose irradiation 
      problematic in intensity-modulated (photon) radiation therapy (IMRT). Here we 
      summarize current evidence on the use of hypofractionated PBT for both 
      early-stage and locally advanced NSCLC, and the possibility of using 
      hypofractionated regimens for patients who are not candidates for concurrent 
      chemotherapy.
