IS  - 2072-1439 (Linking)
VI  - 6
IP  - 5
DP  - 2014 May
TI  - Necrotizing pneumonia and empyema caused by Neisseria flavescens infection.
PG  - 553-7
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2014.02.16 [doi]
AB  - Neisseria flavescens is an uncommon pathogen of human infection, pneumonia and 
      empyema caused by N. flavescens is rarely reported. Herein, we report a 
      56-year-old diabetic patient presenting necrotising pneumonia and empyema due to 
      N. flavescens infection. The main clinical manifestation of this patient was high 
      fever, sticky pus and gradually aggravating dyspnea. The chest computed 
      tomography (CT) scan showed there are mass of high density areas around hilus of 
      the left lung, hollow sign with inflammation also appeared. A biopsy specimen was 
      taken from the left principal bronchus by lung puncture biopsy and showed 
      necrosis and inflammation. Microscopic examination of direct smear and culture of 
      sticky pus, much more gram-negative diplococcus was present, pathogen was further 
      identified by Vitek NH card, Vitek MS and confirmed as N. flavescens by 16S rRNA 
      gene sequencing finally. Anti-infection therapy following the antimicrobial 
      susceptibility test results was effectively. To our knowledge, this is the first 
      report of pulmonary infection caused by N. flavescens.
