TI  - Video-assisted thoracic surgery right upper lobectomy and lymph node dissection.
PG  - S328-30
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2013.08.58 [doi]
AB  - We described a 55-year-old male smoking patient, who came to our institute with 
      the diagnosis of a right upper lobe lesion. Computed tomographic (CT) guided 
      biopsy confirmed a diagnosis of lung adenocarcinoma. The preoperative clinical 
      diagnosis was stage I primary lung adenocarcinoma. The standard three-port 
      video-assisted thoracoscopy surgery (VATS) was performed in this case. After the 
      resection of the right upper lobe, the 2(nd), 4(th), and 7(th) groups of 
      lymphatic nodes were removed with Harmonic scalpel. A closed drainage catheter 
      was placed adjacent to the lateral chest wall through the port in the 7th 
      intercostal space. Postoperative pathologic diagnosis was T2aN0M0 adenocarcinoma.
