TI  - A huge neoplasm occupying the right hemithorax in a pregnancy.
PG  - E237-41
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2014.08.46 [doi]
AB  - Germ cell tumors, like teratoma, typically occur in young adults in their second 
      to fourth decade with equal sex distribution. We firstly report a very rare case 
      of a huge tumor compressed the vital structures of the mediastinum that was 
      diagnosed in a 21-year-old woman at 39 weeks of gestation during a routine 
      prenatal examination. The patient underwent complete en-bloc resection and the 
      size of the tumor was extremely large although no invasion to the vessels or to 
      the airway had occurred. Adherence to the adjacent right pulmonary artery and 
      right main bronchus was present, but without erosion or fistulization. The 
      patient has remained well for over 2 months after the treatment without any signs 
      of disease recurrence.
