DP  - 2012 Feb
TI  - Acute pulmonary embolism after pneumonectomy.
PG  - 76-82
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2011.10.02 [doi]
AB  - Pulmonary embolism (PE) by occlusion of the pulmonary arterial bed may lead to 
      acute life-threatening but potentially reversible right ventricular failure, one 
      of the most severe complications of thoracic surgery. Still, the incidence of 
      acute pulmonary embolism after surgery is reduced by comprehensive anticoagulant 
      prevention, improved surgical techniques, appropriate perioperative management 
      and early ambulation. However, there is difficulty in diagnosing PE after 
      thoracic surgery due to the lack of specific clinical manifestations. So that 
      optimal diagnostic strategy and management according to the clinical presentation 
      and estimated risk of an adverse outcome is fundamental.
