DP  - 2013 Apr
TI  - Vanishing lung mass in a patient with asthma.
PG  - E45-9
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2012.07.01 [doi]
AB  - Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA) is a common but frequently 
      misdiagnosed clinical condition. It is usually diagnosed in patients with a long 
      standing history of asthma. Patients with ABPA can have diverse radiological 
      manifestations. Very rarely, lung masses have been reported. We hereby report a 
      case of ABPA in whom a large symptomatic lung mass was the presenting 
      manifestation leading to consideration of lung cancer as a differential 
      diagnosis. The establishment of ABPA as the underlying diagnosis led to 
      conservative medical treatment which was followed by complete resolution of the 
      mass like opacity. The present case highlights that ABPA should be considered as 
      a differential diagnosis whenever encountering a patient with lung mass and 
      history of asthma.
