TI  - Psoas muscle metastases in non-small cell lung cancer.
PG  - 83-7
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2011.04.05 [doi]
AB  - Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related death in the U.S. and often 
      spreads via lymphatics or through hematogenous metastasis to the brain, bone and 
      adrenal glands. Isolated metastases to skeletal muscle, including the psoas 
      muscles, are very uncommon. The present report is a case series of three patients 
      with psoas metastases from non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) and a review of the 
      relevant literature. Three patients presented with psoas muscle metastases from 
      NSCLC detected on diagnostic imaging. All patients were treated with radiotherapy 
      to the psoas muscle, and two patients were treated with curative intent on an 
      oligometastatic paradigm. Radiotherapy to the psoas muscle was effective and well 
      tolerated.
