DP  - 2012 Oct
TI  - Fibrinous tumor of the pleura: an orphan disease lost in translation.
PG  - 497-503
LID - 10.3978/j.issn.2072-1439.2012.07.09 [doi]
AB  - Fibrous dysplasia is an uncommon, benign disorder also known as fibrous 
      mesothelioma. The cause of fibrous dysplasia is unknown. They represent 5% of all 
      pleura neoplasms and in 80% of all cases arise from the visceral pleura. The 
      epidemiology of the disease is reported equal between males and females around 
      the age of 50. Fibrous dysplasia is usually asymptomatic, although several 
      disease symptoms have been reported as hypoglycemia, pain and swelling may 
      accompany the lesion, in advanced disease. Chemotherapy has not presented disease 
      control; nevertheless, radiotherapy is efficient and indicated in residual 
      disease. The disease progress is usually benign; however several disease 
      manifestations have been reported. There are several molecular pathways, which 
      are possible activated during the disease progress and therefore the disease 
      expression changes throughout its course.
